    </nav>
  </div>

  {{!-- Tab: Toolbar --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'toolbar')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="toolbar" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>Build</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>+</strong> — add a new blank scene node</li>
      <li><strong>Import</strong> — bulk-create nodes from every scene in a folder</li>
      <li><strong>Create Scenes / Update Scenes</strong> — when nodes have no scenes yet the button reads <em>Create Scenes</em> and makes one Foundry scene per node; once scenes exist it reads <em>Update Scenes</em> and re-syncs every node scene with current data</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Navigation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free Move: ON / OFF</strong> — ON lets players navigate freely; OFF makes every move a request the GM must approve</li>
      <li><strong>Requests</strong> — opens the navigation-request drawer; shows a count badge while requests are pending</li>
      <li><strong>Autolock: ON / OFF</strong> — when ON, any node set to <em>Inherit</em> locks players upon arrival</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Utilities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reset ▾</strong> — dropdown with two options: <strong>Reset Macros (n)</strong> resets all "Once" macros so they can fire again (n = pending resets), and <strong>Reset Visited Scenes</strong> clears every player's visited-scene history, re-locking any "scene visited" key conditions</li>
      <li><strong>⚙ Settings</strong> — opens module settings</li>
      <li><strong>Instructions</strong> — opens this help window</li>
      <li>Two more GM-only screens live in Foundry's own <em>Configure Settings → Module Settings</em> rather than this toolbar: <em>Customize HUD Button Style…</em> and <em>Export / Import…</em> (see the <strong>Export / Import</strong> tab)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Canvas --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'canvas')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="canvas" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>View</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><kbd>Right-click</kbd> + drag on background — pan the canvas</li>
      <li><kbd>Mouse wheel</kbd> — zoom in / out toward the cursor</li>
      <li><strong>100%</strong> button (top-left of the workspace) — resets zoom to 1:1 and keeps the current pan position</li>
      <li><strong>Zoom All</strong> button (top-left of the workspace) — fits all nodes into view at the largest zoom that keeps the whole graph visible; useful when the canvas is panned away from all nodes</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Selection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><kbd>Left-click</kbd> + drag on background — draw a selection rectangle; all nodes inside are selected on release</li>
      <li><kbd>Shift</kbd> + drag on background — same as above, but adds to the current selection instead of replacing it</li>
      <li><kbd>Left-click</kbd> on background (no drag) — clear the current selection</li>
      <li><kbd>Shift</kbd> + click a node — add to / remove from the selection</li>
      <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd> — select all nodes</li>
      <li>Drag a selected node — moves the whole selection together</li>
      <li>Drag an unselected node — moves only that node</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Node Actions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Double-click</strong> a node — open its configuration window</li>
      <li><strong>Right-click</strong> a node — teleport menu (only appears while at least one player is online): <em>Send all here</em>, or send an individual player; players already there are marked <em>here</em></li>
      <li><strong>Eye button</strong> (on the node) — View Scene: changes only the GM's view</li>
      <li><strong>Play button</strong> (on the node) — Activate Scene: switches the active scene for everyone. Both buttons are disabled until the node has a scene</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Nodes --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'nodes')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="nodes" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>Creating Nodes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>+</strong> — create a single blank node</li>
      <li><strong>Import</strong> — bulk-create nodes from a scene folder</li>
      <li><strong>Create / Update Scenes</strong> — generate or re-sync the Foundry scene behind each node</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Node Configuration</h3>
    <p>Double-click a node to open its window. A <strong>Default Scene Name</strong> field sits above six tabs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Images</strong> — <em>Add Media</em> (on the tab row) adds images or videos; each entry has a <em>Name</em>, a <em>Set Default</em> button (picks the media shown on the node), <em>Remove Entry</em>, and a macro drop-zone (drop a macro, then Run / Open / Remove and choose its trigger)</li>
      <li><strong>Linked Scenes</strong> — drag a Foundry scene onto the drop-zone to link it; each linked scene has a label, <em>Set Default</em>, <em>Remove Entry</em>, and its own macro drop-zone</li>
      <li><strong>Macros</strong> — drop node-level macros that fire on arrival; per macro set a trigger (<em>GM Activate</em>, <em>Player View</em>, <em>GM View</em>, <em>GM View or Activate</em>) and a mode (<em>Always</em> / <em>Once</em>). A fired "Once" macro shows an <em>Executed</em> badge with a <em>Reset</em> button. A <em>Player View</em> macro runs on the player's own client, so they must own the macro and their role must be allowed to use script macros in Foundry's <em>Configure Permissions</em></li>
      <li><strong>Journal</strong> — drag a journal page (or a whole entry) onto the drop-zone; it opens automatically on arrival, with a trigger of <em>On Scene View</em>, <em>On Scene Activate</em>, or <em>On View or Activate</em>. It writes to the scene's own journal field, and only users the page grants permission to will see it open</li>
      <li><strong>Music</strong> — drag a playlist track (or a whole playlist) onto the drop-zone to set the scene's playlist. Foundry plays it when the scene is <em>activated</em>, not when it is merely viewed from the HUD</li>
      <li><strong>Settings</strong> — <em>Set as Start</em> marks this node as the group's start point, used when activating this group (one per group); <em>Camera Room</em> enables the peek feature (see Camera Room tab); <em>Auto-Lock</em> chooses <em>Open</em>, <em>Inherit</em>, or <em>Locked</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The footer holds <strong>Save</strong> and <strong>Delete Node</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Visual Indicators</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Green border + glow</strong> — this node's scene is currently active</li>
      <li><strong>Red border + glow</strong> — no way out for players: every passage leaving this node is Blocked or Secret (GM-only warning, see the Links tab)</li>
      <li><strong>★ Star icon</strong> — this is this group's start node</li>
      <li><strong>Purple outline + wobble</strong> — node is selected for group move</li>
      <li><strong>Amber outline</strong> — highlighted from the player panel</li>
      <li><strong>Occupant badge + avatars</strong> — players currently in that node</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Links --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'links')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="links" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>Creating Links</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Drag from an anchor dot (edge of a node) to another node — creates a link</li>
      <li>Drop on the node body or on a specific anchor dot</li>
      <li>Hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> while dropping — opens the passage editor immediately</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Interacting with Links</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Click a link — cycle its <strong>direction</strong> (single-passage links only)</li>
      <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd> on Mac) + click a link — cycle its <strong>state</strong> (single-passage links only)</li>
      <li><kbd>Shift</kbd> + click a link — open the passage editor</li>
      <li>Right-click a link — delete (with confirmation)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every passage has a <strong>Direction</strong> and a <strong>State</strong>, shown as two buttons under the <em>Node A ↔ Node B</em> header in the passage editor. Direction decides which side(s) of the link this passage exists on at all; state decides the condition on the side(s) it exists on. A one-way passage's non-existing side isn't "open" or "blocked" — it simply has no route there, nothing is shown, nothing is crossable. Picking the Custom state opens the passage editor right away, since that's the only place its keys can be set.</p>

    <h3>Direction</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>⟷ Both ways</strong> — the passage exists at both ends, same state either way</li>
      <li><strong>→ Forward</strong> — exists only going from the first node to the second; the second node has no route back from this passage</li>
      <li><strong>← Backward</strong> — exists only going from the second node to the first; the first node has no route from this passage</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>State</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Open</strong> — no restriction, a normal route</li>
      <li><strong class="ca-swatch ca-swatch--blocked">⊘ Blocked</strong> — red — players see it but can't cross it</li>
      <li><strong class="ca-swatch ca-swatch--secret"><i class="fa-solid fa-mask"></i> Secret</strong> — purple — hidden from players entirely; the GM sees it marked and can still cross it</li>
      <li><strong class="ca-swatch ca-swatch--custom">🔑 Custom</strong> — blue — visible like Blocked, but only crossable once the player's key conditions are met (see <em>Custom Keys</em> below)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The GM always bypasses Blocked, Secret and Custom. A single passage can't be two different states on its two ends — a door barred on one side and open on the other needs <strong>two passages</strong> on the same link (see <em>Multi-Passage Links</em>): one Forward + Secret, one Backward + Open. That's how a secret passage that's the only way <em>into</em> a room stays hidden, while a separate ordinary passage handles the way back out without ever exposing the secret one on the return trip. The Manager canvas shows an arrow for a one-way passage, a plain ⟷ for Both ways + Open, or a small ⊘ / 🎭 / 🔑 mark when its state isn't Open. A passage that's open one side and Blocked/Secret/Custom the other also gradients from green into that state's color along the line.</p>
    <p>If every passage leaving a node ends up Blocked or Secret, that node has no way out for players — the Manager outlines it in red as a GM-only warning (hover for a tooltip). Nothing is enforced automatically; it's on you to add a route back if you don't want it that way.</p>

    <h3>Custom Keys</h3>
    <p>A Custom-state passage's row in the passage editor grows a keys panel. Drag any of these onto it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>🎒 Item</strong> — player's character must own it</li>
      <li><strong>👤 Actor</strong> — player's character must be it</li>
      <li><strong>⚡ Macro</strong> — must run and return <code>true</code></li>
      <li><strong>🗺️ Scene</strong> — player must have visited it before</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <strong>AND / OR</strong> button decides whether every key is required or just one. No keys set behaves like Open. Keys are only checked when a player actually clicks the destination — not while the HUD is merely open.</p>

    <h3>Multi-Passage Links</h3>
    <p><kbd>Shift</kbd> + click any link to open the passage editor and add multiple named passages between two nodes. Each passage has its own direction and state. Multi-passage links draw as a tight stack of thin dotted lines, one per passage, each colored (or gradiented) by that passage's own direction and state — so a mix of states on the same connection is visible at a glance.</p>

    <h3>Passage Editor</h3>
    <p>Each passage row has five controls:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Name field</strong> — the path label shown to players (e.g. <em>Stairs</em>, <em>Dark Door</em>). Left blank, it is auto-filled as <em>Path 1</em>, <em>Path 2</em>, … on save</li>
      <li><strong>F / P button</strong> — controls how the destination button is labelled in the player HUD:
        <ul>
          <li><strong>F (Full)</strong> — shows <em>Node Name (Path Name)</em>, e.g. <em>Room 3 (Elevator)</em></li>
          <li><strong>P (Path only)</strong> — shows only the path name, e.g. <em>Elevator</em></li>
        </ul>
        The GM always sees the full format regardless of this setting.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Direction button</strong> (⟷ / → / ←) — cycles Both ways / Forward / Backward</li>
      <li><strong>State button</strong> (O / ⊘ / <i class="fa-solid fa-mask"></i> / C) — cycles Open / Blocked / Secret / Custom</li>
      <li><strong>✕ button</strong> — removes the passage row</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The footer holds <strong>Add passage</strong> on the left — plus <strong>Convert to single</strong> on a multi-passage link, which keeps only the first passage and restores simple link behaviour — and <strong>Save</strong> on the right.</p>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Camera Room --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'camera')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="camera" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>What Is a Camera Room?</h3>
    <p>A Camera Room is a node whose players can <strong>peek at other rooms</strong> without navigating to them. The peek swaps the background tile texture on that player's screen only — no scene change, no position update.</p>

    <h3>Setup</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Double-click a node to open its configuration and switch to the <strong>Settings</strong> tab.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Camera: OFF</strong> to toggle it to <strong>Camera: ON</strong>.</li>
      <li>Optionally, fill in the <strong>Button Label</strong> field — this is the text shown on the HUD button for this room (defaults to <em>Cameras</em>). You can rename it to anything (e.g. <em>Monitors</em>, <em>Scrying Pool</em>).</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>As soon as any node in the graph is a camera room, small <strong class="ca-swatch ca-swatch--peek">teal corner anchors</strong> appear at the four corners of every node.</p>

    <h3>Creating Peek Links</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Drag from a <strong class="ca-swatch ca-swatch--peek">teal corner anchor</strong> on a camera room node to a corner anchor on any other node — this creates a <strong>peek link</strong> (teal dashed line with an 👁 marker).</li>
      <li>Corner anchors can only connect to other corner anchors — they cannot be used as regular navigation anchors.</li>
      <li>Right-click a peek link to delete it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Player HUD</h3>
    <p>When a player is located in a camera room that has outgoing peek links:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A button labeled with the room's custom label (default: <em>Cameras</em>) appears in their HUD below the Media button.</li>
      <li>Clicking the button opens a panel listing the peekable rooms and a <strong>Restore View</strong> button.</li>
      <li>Clicking a room swaps the background tile to show that room's image — only for that player, without changing their actual location.</li>
      <li>Clicking <strong>Restore View</strong> cancels the preview and returns to the normal tile of the player's current room.</li>
      <li>Clicking the same room a second time also restores the view.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The preview resets automatically when the player navigates to another node or when the scene reloads.</p>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Players --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'players')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="players" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>Player Panel</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Players are grouped into <strong>Game Master</strong>, <strong>Online</strong>, and <strong>Offline</strong></li>
      <li>Hover a player row — highlights their current node on the canvas</li>
      <li>Hover a node — highlights the rows of players in that node</li>
      <li>Click a player row — centers the canvas on that player's node</li>
      <li><strong>Lock button</strong> on each row — locks or unlocks that single player</li>
      <li><strong>Lock All / Unlock All</strong> (panel header icons) — toggle every player at once</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Teleporting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Right-click a node to open the teleport menu (only while a player is online)</li>
      <li><strong>Send all here</strong> — moves every online player to that node</li>
      <li>Pick a single player — moves just them; players already there are marked <em>here</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Autolock</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Autolock</strong> ON (toolbar) locks players on arrival at any node set to <em>Inherit</em></li>
      <li>Per-node override (node Settings tab): <em>Open</em>, <em>Locked</em>, or <em>Inherit</em></li>
      <li>Locked players cannot request navigation — the GM must send or unlock them</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Pause &amp; Lock</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Pausing the game (Foundry pause button) <strong>locks all players</strong> — none can navigate while paused</li>
      <li>Unpausing <strong>restores each player's previous lock state</strong> — manually locked players stay locked; free players regain movement</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Navigation Modes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free Move ON</strong> — players move freely without GM approval</li>
      <li><strong>Free Move OFF</strong> — each move sends a request the GM approves or rejects</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Navigation Requests (Free Move OFF)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Pending requests appear in the drawer (Requests button in the toolbar)</li>
      <li>Approve or reject individual requests, or approve all at once</li>
      <li>A new request from the same player overwrites their previous one</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Groups --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'groups')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="groups" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>What Is a Group?</h3>
    <p>A group is one complete, self-contained scene graph — its own nodes, its own links, and its own start node. A world can hold as many as you like, but the Manager and the player HUD always work on the <strong>active</strong> group; the others sit dormant and untouched. Use them to keep separate adventures, chapters, or a dungeon and the town above it apart from one another.</p>

    <h3>Opening the Group Manager</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Groups</strong> button — in the <em>Scenes</em> sidebar header, next to the <em>Click Adventure</em> button</li>
      <li>Or a script macro: <kbd>ClickAdventure.Groups();</kbd></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The List</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The group currently in play carries an <strong>Active</strong> badge and a green edge</li>
      <li><strong>Activate</strong> — makes that group the active one and reopens the Manager on it. Every online player is moved to the group's start node, but <em>only</em> if that start node already has a Foundry scene; a new or scene-less group still activates and players simply stay where they are</li>
      <li><strong>Pencil</strong> — rename the group</li>
      <li><strong>Trash</strong> — delete the group. It also removes that group's own scene folder and the scenes inside it, so nothing is left orphaned, and it cannot be undone. The button is disabled for the active group and whenever only one group exists — there is always at least one group, and you can never delete the one you are standing in</li>
      <li><strong>New Group</strong> — creates an empty group; you are asked for a name and offered <em>New Group 1</em>, <em>2</em>, … as a default</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Before Activating</h3>
    <p>Set the destination group's start node first (node <em>Settings</em> tab → <em>Set as Start</em>) and run <strong>Create Scenes</strong> for it. Activating a group whose start node has no scene leaves everyone standing in the old adventure, which looks like nothing happened.</p>
  </section>

  {{!-- Tab: Export / Import --}}
  <section class="ca-instr-panel {{#if (eq activeTab 'io')}}ca-instr-panel--active{{/if}}" data-panel="io" role="tabpanel">
    <h3>Where It Lives</h3>
    <p>Export and import are GM-only and sit in Foundry's own settings, not in the Manager toolbar: <em>Configure Settings → Module Settings → Click Adventure → Export / Import…</em></p>

    <h3>Exporting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Tick the groups to include — <strong>All</strong> and <strong>None</strong> set the whole list at once</li>
      <li><strong>Export</strong> downloads a JSON file named <em>click-adventure-export-&lt;timestamp&gt;.json</em></li>
      <li>The file carries each group's nodes, links, images, linked scenes, macros, journal, music, and camera room settings. It does <strong>not</strong> carry the image files themselves — a world that imports it needs those assets at the same paths</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Importing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Choose file…</strong> picks an exported JSON, then tick the groups you want and press <strong>Import</strong>; <strong>Cancel</strong> backs out without touching the world</li>
      <li>Imported groups are <strong>added</strong> to the world — nothing existing is replaced or overwritten</li>
      <li>Foundry scenes are created automatically for the imported nodes</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How References Survive the Trip</h3>
    <p>Macros, journals, playlists and linked scenes are written to the file with both their ID and their name. On import each one is resolved by ID first — which matches instantly when the file came from this same world — and falls back to matching by name anywhere else.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A journal whose page is missing keeps the entry, and the whole entry opens instead</li>
      <li>A playlist whose track is missing keeps the playlist, and Foundry follows the playlist's own playback mode</li>
      <li>Both of those raise a warning rather than failing; only a missing root document clears the reference entirely</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</div>
